---
import Layout from '~/layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import Hero from '~/components/widgets/Hero.astro';
import Content from '~/components/widgets/Content.astro';
import Features3 from '~/components/widgets/Features3.astro';
import CallToAction from '~/components/widgets/CallToAction.astro';
import YouTubeEmbed from '~/components/widgets/YouTubeEmbed.astro';

const metadata = {
  title: 'Peer to Peer: Pair Programming with Epic React — Masterschool',
};
---

<Layout metadata={metadata}>
  <Hero tagline="Mentor · Presentation · Instructional Design · Masterschool · 2022">
    <Fragment slot="title">
      Peer to Peer —<br />
      <span class="text-accent dark:text-white">Pair Programming with Epic React</span>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="subtitle">
      A live presentation delivered to Masterschool students introducing pair programming as a structured learning
      strategy — covering the driver/navigator model, the benefits and challenges of working in pairs, and how to get
      the most out of collaborative coding sessions in Epic React.
    </Fragment>
  </Hero>

  <!-- Context -->
  <Content
    tagline="The Presentation"
    title="Teaching students how to learn together, not just alongside each other"
    isReversed
  >
    <Fragment slot="image">
      <div class="flex items-center justify-center h-full py-12">
        <div class="text-center">
          <p class="text-7xl font-bold font-heading text-accent leading-none">Peer<br />to<br />Peer</p>
          <p class="text-xl text-muted mt-3">Masterschool</p>
          <div class="mt-8 space-y-2 text-sm text-muted">
            <p class="font-semibold text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">Mentor · Instructional Design</p>
            <p>Live Student Presentation</p>
            <p>2022</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="content">
      <p class="mb-4">
        As a mentor at Masterschool, part of my role was not just supporting students through technical content — it was
        helping them develop the habits and strategies that make learning stick over time. One of those strategies is
        pair programming, which the Epic React curriculum incorporates as a structured practice.
      </p>
      <p class="mb-4">
        Before students broke into pairs for the Epic React Testing Workshop, I delivered a live presentation
        introducing pair programming as a learning methodology — not just a workflow. The goal was to give students a
        framework for thinking about how they learn, why active struggle is more valuable than passive consumption, and
        how collaboration can accelerate both understanding and output.
      </p>
      <p>
        The presentation drew on adult learning principles: the difference between passive and active learning, the
        psychology of productive struggle, and the practical mechanics of the driver/navigator model.
      </p>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="bg">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 section-pattern-b"></div>
    </Fragment>
  </Content>

  <!-- Key Topics -->
  <Features3
    tagline="What the Presentation Covered"
    title="A framework for learning together, not just working together"
    subtitle="The presentation was structured to move students from learning theory into practical pair programming strategy before the workshop began."
    columns={3}
    items={[
      {
        title: 'Two Types of Learning',
        description:
          'Framing the difference between passive learning (watching tutorials, reading articles) and active learning (coding challenges, projects with code reviews) — and why the hard path produces more durable skills.',
        icon: 'tabler:brain',
      },
      {
        title: 'The Driver & Navigator Model',
        description:
          'Introducing the core pair programming structure: one person drives (controls the keyboard), one person navigates (explains the strategy). Switching roles every 25 minutes to share both perspectives.',
        icon: 'tabler:steering-wheel',
      },
      {
        title: 'Benefits of Pairing',
        description:
          'Two heads are better than one for problem-solving. Pairing builds accountability, reduces shortcuts, improves focus, and creates natural opportunities to catch bugs and share knowledge across different skill sets.',
        icon: 'tabler:users',
      },
      {
        title: 'Navigating the Challenges',
        description:
          "Honest discussion of where pair programming gets hard — working with partners at different skill levels, feeling like you're slowing each other down, disagreeing on approach — and how to communicate through it.",
        icon: 'tabler:alert-triangle',
      },
      {
        title: 'Communication First',
        description:
          'Emphasizing the importance of talking before coding — discussing strategy, sharing ideas freely, and treating the session as a collaborative problem-solving exercise rather than a race to write code.',
        icon: 'tabler:message-circle',
      },
      {
        title: 'Growth Mindset',
        description:
          "Closing with a reframe: being a developer isn't about mastering a fixed set of skills. The tools and languages matter less than the general skill of solving problems — and that skill develops through practice, repetition, and collaboration.",
        icon: 'tabler:trending-up',
      },
    ]}
  />

  <!-- Download -->
  <Content tagline="Presentation Materials" title="Download the slide deck">
    <Fragment slot="content">
      <p class="mb-6">
        The full slide deck from the Peer to Peer presentation is available as a PDF — covering all topics from learning
        theory through the Epic React Testing Workshop introduction.
      </p>
      <a
        href="/downloads/Pair-Programming-Epic-React.pdf"
        download="Pair-Programming-Epic-React.pdf"
        class="inline-flex items-center gap-3 bg-accent text-white font-semibold px-6 py-3 rounded-lg hover:bg-accent/90 transition-colors"
      >
        <svg
          xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
          class="w-5 h-5"
          viewBox="0 0 24 24"
          fill="none"
          stroke="currentColor"
          stroke-width="2"
          stroke-linecap="round"
          stroke-linejoin="round"
          ><path d="M21 15v4a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H5a2 2 0 0 1-2-2v-4"></path><polyline points="7 10 12 15 17 10"
          ></polyline><line x1="12" y1="15" x2="12" y2="3"></line></svg
        >
        Download Slide Deck (PDF)
      </a>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="bg">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 section-pattern-d"></div>
    </Fragment>
  </Content>

  <!-- Video -->
  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 md:px-8 py-12 md:py-16">
    <div class="text-center mb-10">
      <p class="text-base text-secondary dark:text-blue-200 font-bold tracking-wide uppercase mb-2">
        Presentation Recording
      </p>
      <h2 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold tracking-tighter font-heading">Watch the Presentation</h2>
      <p class="text-lg text-muted mt-4">
        Live presentation delivered to Masterschool students on pair programming with Epic React, 2022.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold mb-3">Peer to Peer: Pair Programming with Epic React</h3>
      <YouTubeEmbed id="RRj90JbkjP0" title="Peer to Peer: Pair Programming with Epic React — Masterschool" />
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Reflection -->
  <Content
    tagline="Reflection"
    title="Teaching learning strategies is as important as teaching technical content"
    isReversed
  >
    <Fragment slot="image">
      <div class="flex items-center justify-center h-full py-12">
        <div class="text-center">
          <p class="text-8xl font-bold font-heading text-accent leading-none">2022</p>
          <p class="text-xl text-muted mt-3">Masterschool</p>
          <div class="mt-8 space-y-2 text-sm text-muted">
            <p class="font-semibold text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">Mentor · Instructional Design</p>
            <p>Live Student Presentation</p>
            <p>Epic React Testing Workshop</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="content">
      <p class="mb-4">
        What this presentation reinforced is something that holds across every technical training context: learners need
        to understand how to learn, not just what to learn. The mechanics of pair programming are straightforward — one
        person drives, one navigates, switch every 25 minutes. But the underlying principle is about productive
        struggle: staying in the discomfort of not-knowing long enough to build real understanding, rather than reaching
        for shortcuts that feel like progress.
      </p>
      <p class="mb-4">
        That same principle applies directly to AI adoption training in a workplace. When employees are learning to use
        new tools like AI assistants, the temptation is the same — to copy a working prompt without understanding why it
        works, or to avoid the tool entirely when it doesn't behave as expected. The instructional design challenge is
        the same: create an environment where struggle is reframed as progress, and where collaboration accelerates
        learning rather than short-circuiting it.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-2">
        {
          [
            'Pair Programming',
            'Active Learning',
            'Growth Mindset',
            'Live Presentation',
            'Masterschool',
            'Epic React',
            'Instructional Design',
            'Adult Learning',
            'Technical Communication',
            'Collaborative Learning',
          ].map((tag) => (
            <span class="bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-900 text-blue-800 dark:text-blue-200 text-sm font-medium px-3 py-1 rounded-full">
              {tag}
            </span>
          ))
        }
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-muted mt-4">
        Mentor & Instructional Design, Masterschool — Peer to Peer: Pair Programming with Epic React, 2022.
      </p>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="bg">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 section-pattern"></div>
    </Fragment>
  </Content>

  <CallToAction
    actions={[
      { variant: 'primary', text: 'View All Projects', href: '/projects' },
      { text: 'Get in Touch', href: '/about#contact' },
    ]}
  >
    <Fragment slot="title">Interested in working together?</Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="subtitle"
      >I'm open to remote opportunities in instructional design and technical learning experience design.</Fragment
    >
  </CallToAction>
</Layout>
